<Window x:Class="Jrpg.Editor.Shell.ControlsHelpWindow"
        x:ClassModifier="internal"
        Style="{StaticResource EditorWindow}"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Controls"
        Width="560"
        Height="640"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        ShowInTaskbar="False">
  <!-- Every gesture and key the editor has; a slice that adds one adds its line here. -->
  <Window.Resources>
    <Style x:Key="Heading" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,12,0,4" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="Lines" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
    </Style>
  </Window.Resources>
  <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
    <StackPanel Margin="12,0,12,12">
      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Heading}" Text="Anywhere" />
      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Lines}">
        Ctrl+N: new map.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl+S: save the shown map and the tiles.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl+Shift+S: save every changed map and the tiles and objects, checked together, all or none (File &gt; Save all); Ctrl+S checks the other maps as saved, and says when Save all would pass. Closing with unsaved changes: Save all, Don't save or Cancel.<LineBreak />
        Maps stay open: switch freely, a * in the map list marks unsaved changes.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl+M: filter the map list by name or id; Enter opens the first map shown, Esc clears it.<LineBreak />
        Alt+Left: back to the previous map; again to return.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y: undo / redo the shown map's changes and the tiles' and objects', newest first; each open map keeps its own.<LineBreak />
        F1: this list.
      </TextBlock>

      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Heading}" Text="View" />
      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Lines}">
        Middle drag, or Space + drag: pan.<LineBreak />
        Arrow keys: pan, while the map has focus.<LineBreak />
        Mouse wheel: zoom toward the mouse.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl+0: fit the map. Ctrl+1: actual size.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl+H: show collision: cells the player can't enter (red), object outlines (green), above-player zones (blue).<LineBreak />
        The layer list chooses the layer edits go to; Add layer adds one at a preset or any z. Dim and lock other layers (tool bar, View menu): the other layers are drawn faint and ignore every click, in the Objects and Events tabs, so an object under another layer's object is reached once its layer is chosen; the Placed object panel's note says when the lock left a click with nothing.
      </TextBlock>

      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Heading}" Text="Tiles" />
      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Lines}">
        Click a cell in the Tiles panel: choose it; it becomes a tile the first time it is painted.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl + mouse wheel over the sheet: zoom toward the mouse. − and + over the sheet: smaller or bigger cells; the size between them goes back to the map's 48 px. The composer's picker shares the zoom.<LineBreak />
        Drag over the sheet (Shift + drag works too): select a rectangle of cells (dashed); painting uses its top-left cell, and Objects > New object starts from them.<LineBreak />
        Left drag: paint with the chosen tool. Right drag: erase.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl + drag on the map: select a rectangle of map cells (dashed); Ctrl + click: one cell; Esc clears it.<LineBreak />
        B: brush (paints as you drag). R: rectangle (filled on release). F: fill (click).<LineBreak />
        Shift + click: fill, with any tool.<LineBreak />
        Alt + click: pick the tile and layer under the mouse.<LineBreak />
        Set up (Tiles panel or menu), on an image "(not set up)": open the Cut panel with a guessed cut; set columns, rows, margin and spacing, then Apply.<LineBreak />
        Import... (Tiles panel or menu): copy PNG images into the tilesheet art; the last one is shown, ready to set up. Not undone by Ctrl+Z.<LineBreak />
        Cut (Tiles panel or menu), on a set-up sheet: re-cut it in the Cut panel; its tiles keep their column and row.<LineBreak />
        Mark blocked (Tiles tool bar or menu): while on, a click on a sheet cell flips it between walkable and blocked (red cross).<LineBreak />
        [ and ]: smaller / larger brush.<LineBreak />
        Fill layer / Clear layer: the whole chosen layer.
      </TextBlock>

      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Heading}" Text="Objects" />
      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Lines}">
        Click: place the chosen object, centered on the mouse.<LineBreak />
        Shift + drag: move an object; on a selected object, move the whole selection.<LineBreak />
        Alt, held while clicking or Shift + dragging: snap the object's top-left to the grid (the ghost shows where). Snap to grid in the Objects tool bar, or G: snap by default; Alt then places freely.<LineBreak />
        Right click: remove an object.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl + drag: select objects.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl + click: select the object under the mouse (on empty ground: select nothing); again on the same spot: the next object under it, a roof counting as drawn over what it hides, wrapping to the top (the Placed object panel's Next under does the same, and its note says "2 of 3 objects here"). Shift + drag moves the selected object under the mouse before the one on top. Esc, with the map clicked: clear the selection.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl+C / Ctrl+X: copy / cut the selected objects with their events. Ctrl+V: paste them centered on the mouse, or on the middle of the view from the Edit menu (Alt snaps), on this map or another; the pasted ones are selected.<LineBreak />
        Placed object (under the list, with one object selected): change its object, X, Y or layer and press Apply or Enter (one Ctrl+Z step); its uid and events stay. Edit events... opens its events.<LineBreak />
        Arrow keys, with objects selected: move them one cell; Shift + arrow: one art pixel. Each press is one Ctrl+Z step. With nothing selected, arrows pan.<LineBreak />
        Delete: remove the selected objects, or the one under the mouse.<LineBreak />
        Objects > Edit shapes: open the shapes window for the one placed object selected, else the object chosen in the Objects panel.<LineBreak />
        Shift + double-click a placed object, or double-click one in the Objects panel: open its shapes.<LineBreak />
        Objects > New object from map selection (or From map... in the tool bar): the composer on the cells selected with Ctrl + drag (Tiles tab); the chosen layer becomes level 0, each layer under or over it with a tile there the next level down or up. Replace the cells (ticked): Save also erases the cells the object kept, from the chosen layer up, and places it there; Ctrl+Z undoes the replacement, then the object.<LineBreak />
        Objects > New object: the composer; choose cells on its sheet, click or drag on the canvas to stamp them, right click or drag to erase (or the Erase toggle), Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y undo strokes; Save, then its shapes open.<LineBreak />
        In the composer, Ctrl + mouse wheel over the canvas, or − and + over it: zoom the canvas.<LineBreak />
        In the composer, the Level box chooses the level strokes paint and erase, from -7 to 7 (0 is the base; a higher level is drawn over a lower one; the levels above the chosen one are faded; a • marks the levels painted on).<LineBreak />
        In the composer, drag the bar between the sheet and the canvas to widen either.<LineBreak />
        Objects > Edit object: the composer on the object chosen in the Objects panel (not an NPC); Save keeps its id, outline, zones, placements and events, and opens its shapes when its size changed.<LineBreak />
        Objects > Duplicate object (or Duplicate... in the tool bar): the composer on a copy of the chosen object's picture, with its outline, zones and category; type the new id, repaint what differs, Save makes the new object and opens its shapes when its size changed.<LineBreak />
        Objects > New NPC: a one-cell NPC drawn from a field sprite (the placeholder by default); place it, then give it a Talk event.
      </TextBlock>

      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Heading}" Text="Shapes window" />
      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Lines}">
        Collision or Over the player: what clicks draw, the collision outline (green) or the chosen zone (blue).<LineBreak />
        New zone / Delete zone: zones are the parts drawn over the player (leaves, roofs); a click with no zone chosen starts one.<LineBreak />
        Click: add a point to the chosen shape (kept inside the object, on its pixels); near an edge, insert one there.<LineBreak />
        Drag a handle: move its point. Right click a handle: remove its point. The yellow handle is the last point.<LineBreak />
        Shift + drag: make the chosen shape a rectangle. Whole object: the full object. Snap to 1/4 tile: points land on quarter cells.<LineBreak />
        Mouse wheel: zoom (0.5x to 12x). Shapes not being edited are drawn faint.<LineBreak />
        Backspace or Ctrl+Z: remove the last point. Clear shape: no outline (the object never blocks).<LineBreak />
        OK: apply, as one change Ctrl+Z undoes in the main window. Esc or Cancel: leave the object as it was.
      </TextBlock>

      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Heading}" Text="Events" />
      <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Lines}">
        Click an object: edit its events. Presets: Chest, Door, Door you open, Sign, and Talk (an NPC: type its speaker and lines).<LineBreak />
        Click a cell without an object: edit the event that runs when the player walks onto it.<LineBreak />
        Right click a cell without an object: remove its event.<LineBreak />
        Ctrl + click: the event of the cell under any object (a new one when it has none); Ctrl + right click: remove it (a plain right click never removes an event hidden under an object).<LineBreak />
        A warp step's Pick...: choose the map on the left, click the arrival cell on its picture (double-click: choose and OK); Ctrl + wheel or − and + zoom it. A cell the player can't stand on, or holding a walk-into event, is warned about.<LineBreak />
        A warp step's Go to: keep the events, then show the destination map centered on its cell (gold square). Alt+Left comes back.<LineBreak />
        On the map, in every mode: a yellow dot is an event run when pressed, a cyan dot one run when walked into, a cyan square a cell with an event.
      </TextBlock>
    </StackPanel>
  </ScrollViewer>
</Window>
